From 88724cab0cf3fae36ea47e8c9b547c5655319e78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonny Heggheim <hegjon@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 16:16:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 03/12] libalpm: btrpkg downloads in pacman's sandbox, with
 real sizes and progress

The network half (btrpkg fetch-ids) runs as DownloadUser under pacman's
seccomp filter and its own Landlock ruleset, writing only to
CACHEDIR/download-btrpkg. pull-ids runs as root without network: it
checks everything against the database's IDs, reuses installed and
cached data, names the chunks still missing, and assembles the cache
files once fetch-ids has them. Root never writes into the download
user's directory.

- CacheServer entries first, then Server; each server is asked for what
  the previous one lacked or delivered damaged.
- An interrupted download keeps its directory; the next run fetches
  only what is missing. -Sc/-Scc remove the leftovers (nftw, FTW_PHYS).
- Download size: the indexes are fetched in _alpm_sync_prepare and
  pull-ids --plan reports what is really missing, so the summary and
  the download space check use it.
- Progress: fetch-ids' progress lines become download callbacks.
